WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:12.633
Every app you use asks who you are. If each one keeps its own passwords, you have ten passwords, and whoever runs them has ten places to add or remove a person.

00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:28.590
Keycloak is one place that knows who you are. The apps never see your password: they send you to Keycloak and trust its answer. The lab has two machines: a server with Keycloak, and a client with a small web app.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:39.295
One command installs both, from cloud images. I will go fast here.

00:00:39.395 --> 00:00:46.103
This is the app. It has no login form of its own: just a Login button.

00:00:46.203 --> 00:00:55.834
The button brings you here, to Keycloak. The password goes to Keycloak, never to the app. I sign in as Alice.

00:00:55.934 --> 00:01:03.424
Alice is in, and the app knows it without ever seeing her password. The protected page opens.

00:01:03.524 --> 00:01:09.587
The editor page is for people with the role editor. Alice has it: it opens.

00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:16.389
I log out, and sign in as Bob.

00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:29.693
Bob signed in just like Alice, but he has no editor role: 403, forbidden. This is the difference between authentication, who you are, and authorization, what you may do.

00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:43.193
Behind all this is the server. Its admin console is where Alice, Bob and the role editor were created.

00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:51.623
A realm is a world of its own, with its users and its apps. Ours is called lab: two users.

00:01:51.723 --> 00:02:03.709
And the registered apps. lab-web is ours, with the address Keycloak may send the browser back to. An app that is not in this list cannot ask for a login.

00:02:03.809 --> 00:02:13.677
The same things can be done from the command line, with kcadm. Here I give Bob the role editor.

00:02:13.777 --> 00:02:22.847
Now Bob has it. And I take it back at once: in the lab, Bob stays without.

00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:38.243
What did the app receive instead of a password? A token: a signed ticket from Keycloak. Inside: who issued it, for whom, until when, and the roles. The app checks the signature and trusts it.

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:50.345
How does the app know the keys that check the signature? It asks Keycloak, at a public address: the discovery.

00:02:50.445 --> 00:03:02.629
Not only people: a program can identify itself too, with a secret of its own. This token lives five seconds, and right now it is active.

00:03:02.729 --> 00:03:10.638
Six seconds later it is worth nothing. A stolen token is worth little too.

00:03:10.738 --> 00:03:18.345
And with a wrong secret, Keycloak says no: 401.

00:03:18.445 --> 00:03:32.013
The lab has seven tests that do all of this on their own, the login included, and tell you if it still works.

00:03:32.113 --> 00:03:42.350 line:6%
Commands and explanations are in the guide, in English and Italian, from the lab's card.
